1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,240
Amen.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:54,820
Well, thank you.

3
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:56,560
Thank you.

4
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:01,260
And thank you all for joining us this morning.

5
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:05,280
If we haven't had the privilege of meeting yet, my name is Ryan, and I'm one of the pastors

6
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:11,620
here, and I'm going to need to get some more volume, I think, maybe.

7
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:12,280
Can you guys hear me?

8
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:13,320
Oh, okay.

9
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:19,560
We are continuing our series through the book of Psalms this summer, and it's no accident

10
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:24,980
that we spent so much time meditating on the significance of the cross this morning as

11
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:27,920
we are turning our Bibles to Psalm chapter 51.

12
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:30,480
So if you want to turn there with me, you can.

13
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:36,000
Psalm 51 is one of the more famous Psalms in the book of Psalms, and it is a Psalm of confession,

14
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:47,440
a Psalm of David after some of the most horrendous, horrible sins that we could imagine committing

15
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:54,740
before a holy God when David found himself in a position of leadership over the people of God.

16
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:02,240
And so we need to remind ourselves, we need to meditate on the power of the cross this morning

17
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:07,760
because it is the only way forgiveness can be found for those of us who have fallen and

18
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:08,340
sinned.

19
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:16,180
Whether it be at the level of David or any other form of sin, we have all fallen short of God's

20
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:17,040
glory and his grace.

21
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:21,940
And so this morning, may the cross just be in the back of our minds as we cover this Psalm

22
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:22,800
this morning.

23
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:28,040
I am going to share a story with you all about my mother.

24
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:30,100
I asked her permission.

25
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:35,980
And I also, I asked her not to give me all the details of the story because the story,

26
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:39,620
the way that I remember it makes an incredible sermon illustration.

27
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:45,220
And I was afraid that if I called and talked her through all the details, that she would

28
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:48,800
correct me on some things that would make it less of a good sermon illustration.

29
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:51,940
So I'm taking some creative liberties here.

30
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:56,480
And if part of this story isn't completely accurate, just go with me because it's a good

31
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:57,300
sermon illustration.

32
00:02:57,300 --> 00:02:57,760
All right.

33
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:05,800
About a year ago, Marissa and I were taking Rory to our denominational church conference.

34
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:07,560
And Marissa was going to work remotely.

35
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:10,720
I was going to be at the conference and we were going to need some help with the baby.

36
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:11,680
If we were going to pull it off.

37
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:15,480
So we got an Airbnb, invited my parents down to come help me.

38
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,660
They're lovely people in the retirement phase of their lives, able to help, willing to help

39
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:21,320
with our child.

40
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:29,140
And what happened is as they got there, I realized that they weren't going to be very helpful.

41
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:37,340
My mom was practically bedridden with back pain and love her to death.

42
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:42,780
Yes, she came anyway with like able hands, willing to do whatever she could when she could stand

43
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:44,400
up, which wasn't very often.

44
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:50,280
And so she was just crippled with this back pain and unable to do just about anything that

45
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,180
she wanted to do.

46
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:53,880
And she'd been wrestling with this back pain for a long time.

47
00:03:53,880 --> 00:04:00,660
And finally, through different types of treatment and different investigative processes, one doctor

48
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:07,760
found out that the cause of her back pain was actually the result of a heel spur on her foot.

49
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:14,740
It was because she was limping because of this heel spur on her foot and that jacked her back

50
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:15,200
all up.

51
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:19,780
And then that led to all of this pain that on the surface looked like back pain.

52
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:24,180
But in order to really address the back pain, we were going to have to address the heel spur,

53
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:24,500
right?

54
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,300
That sounds like some of you all are in the medical field.

55
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:29,420
Sounds pretty normal, right?

56
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:32,640
Okay, so here's where it gets really interesting.

57
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:36,900
The doctor who told her that she was going to have to get this heel spur figured out.

58
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:41,900
Well, apparently in order to fix the heel spur, you have to detach the Achilles tendon.

59
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:45,180
That doesn't sound minor to me.

60
00:04:45,180 --> 00:04:46,200
Does that sound minor to you?

61
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:51,760
At that point, I'm thinking about my back pain and thinking about how qualified this doctor

62
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:57,480
might be to detach one of my attendants and then retouch them and the recovery process that

63
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:58,160
that might take.

64
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:03,540
And I'm like, hmm, maybe, maybe the back pain isn't as bad as I thought it was, right?

65
00:05:03,540 --> 00:05:06,580
Like maybe we don't have to address this right now.

66
00:05:06,580 --> 00:05:07,600
Maybe he's wrong.

67
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:11,400
Maybe we can look into this from a couple of different angles.

68
00:05:12,460 --> 00:05:17,840
And so finally, my mom decided to get this very painful procedure on her foot that had

69
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:24,340
a long time of recovery, but ultimately led to healing and restoration of her whole body.

70
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:29,860
And I share this illustration before we hop into a text about confession, because I think

71
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:32,380
that's how confession works in the life of a believer.

72
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:40,280
That in order to really get full restoration, full healing, we have to go through this painful

73
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:44,380
process of confession before God and others.

74
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:47,700
And the reality is it's not our only option, right?

75
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:50,300
Like my mom didn't have to go through this surgery.

76
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:51,600
She had a couple of options.

77
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:53,520
She could have managed the pain.

78
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:58,320
There's some good pills out there, some good gummies.

79
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:07,380
Some bottles that people have turned to in the midst of pain.

80
00:06:07,380 --> 00:06:10,620
Like you can manage pain if you want to.

81
00:06:10,620 --> 00:06:15,020
If you don't want to address the core issue that's involved in your life, you can manage

82
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:15,500
the pain.

83
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:20,580
The other option is she could have just given in to the fact that she's a crippled person

84
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:21,960
now, right?

85
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:26,300
She could have owned this identity that I am a person with a back that does not work.

86
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:28,240
I'm just going to sit in this.

87
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:33,240
And I think the same is true with us when we are in the midst of sin and rebellion before

88
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:33,500
God.

89
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:34,620
We've got some options.

90
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:37,220
We can manage the pain.

91
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:38,240
We can cover it up.

92
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:39,080
We can hide.

93
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:48,000
We can sulk and sit in our shame and just say, this is who I am.

94
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,140
I am a broken person.

95
00:06:49,140 --> 00:06:50,140
I will never change.

96
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:51,540
I will never be able to change.

97
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:52,540
This is who I am.

98
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:53,620
This is my identity now.

99
00:06:53,620 --> 00:06:59,700
Those are some options, but the option that the Scripture gives us, the option that we

100
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:05,420
see in the life of David is this willingness to undergo this painful process of confession

101
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:10,640
that ultimately leads to restoration and healing of the body.

102
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:13,280
This is what we're going to see in our psalm today.

103
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:19,600
God, the loving physician, he sends the prophet Nathan to confront David with the realities of

104
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:20,420
his pain management.

105
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:26,660
He loves David enough to say, you're not as good as you think you are.

106
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:31,540
And you might be able to manage this for a while, but ultimately, if you want true joy,

107
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:35,020
if you want true healing, if you want true life and fulfillment, you're going to have to

108
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:37,440
deal with what's going on inside of your soul.

109
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:45,260
The prophet Nathan helps David to see the chronic issue in his life rooted in unconfessed sin.

110
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:52,580
And the result of that confrontation is this incredibly beautiful, powerful, and prophetic prayer of confession

111
00:07:52,580 --> 00:08:00,460
that is probably one of the most useful psalms to the community of faith for the past thousand or two thousand years.

112
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:08,840
And the reason it's one of the most useful psalms to the people of God is I don't know what your experience is with the people of God,

113
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:18,220
but in my experience with the people of God throughout centuries is that we have a propensity to mess up and cover up instead of fessing up.

114
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:22,540
This is what we do as a people.

115
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:26,720
And many of us have experienced this in our own lives.

116
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:32,160
Many of us have caused more damage in our lives than needed to be caused because we messed up,

117
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:36,880
and instead of dealing with the consequences of our sin, we covered them up, we hid them,

118
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:42,220
and that led to more sin, more lying, more betraying, and more pain.

119
00:08:42,220 --> 00:08:43,040
Thank you.

120
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:49,480
And some of us, other people have done this in our lives.

121
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:56,520
Some of us are victims of unconfessed sin, unrepentant sin, and we have been caught in the web of somebody else's lies,

122
00:08:56,620 --> 00:09:03,520
and there's so much additional pain that comes from this because we try and manage the pain rather than go to the great physician

123
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:12,400
and allow him to address the root cause of our pain and give us whole body, whole soul, whole mind restoration and healing.

124
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:18,320
We need this in our lives.

125
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:28,340
So, let's unpack this psalm section by section with the hope of seeing that confession is a painful participation in grace

126
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:36,060
rooted in God's character that leads to the restoration of individuals and the authenticity of communities.

127
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:37,340
I think that's going to be on the screen.

128
00:09:37,340 --> 00:09:42,620
And I worked really hard on this, so what I'm going to need you to do is to at least pretend like you're writing it down.

129
00:09:42,620 --> 00:09:44,560
Do we have it?

130
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:45,700
Maybe.

131
00:09:45,700 --> 00:09:46,780
Maybe not.

132
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:51,240
I'll say it again, and you pretend, everybody pretend like you're writing it down, okay?

133
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:56,720
Confession is a painful participation in grace.

134
00:09:56,720 --> 00:10:05,860
It's rooted in God's character, and it leads to the restoration of individuals and the authenticity of communities.

135
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:08,660
You guys didn't even pretend.

136
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:09,980
I took a picture.

137
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:11,140
There we go, there we go.

138
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:11,980
Pictures, that's great.

139
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:13,820
Thank you, thank you.

140
00:10:13,820 --> 00:10:17,700
I'm not trying to coddle my own ego here or anything, but.

141
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:18,900
Yeah, I am.

142
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:19,400
All right, cool.

143
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:23,200
All right, first, let's unpack this first section.

144
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:27,280
It's rooted in God's character.

145
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:31,140
Psalm 51, verse 1.

146
00:10:31,140 --> 00:10:32,660
We'll read the script above.

147
00:10:32,660 --> 00:10:33,740
It says,

148
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:41,760
So the director of the prophet Nathan came to him after David had committed adultery with Bathsheba.

149
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:45,920
So if you don't know the whole backstory, it's in 2 Samuel 11 and 12.

150
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:48,060
Feel free to go back and read some of that.

151
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:54,440
David, the king of Israel commits adultery with a woman who is married to one of his close soldiers.

152
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:56,900
She gets pregnant, and he freaks out.

153
00:10:56,900 --> 00:11:04,660
And so he tries to get the soldier to come back from war, to sleep with his wife, so nobody knows it's David's baby.

154
00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:10,060
Maybe that doesn't work because Uriah is a man of character, unlike David at this point in their lives.

155
00:11:10,060 --> 00:11:14,380
And David, continuing to try and cover up his sin, has Uriah killed.

156
00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:22,780
And ultimately, through having Uriah killed, has multiple soldiers who wouldn't have been killed in battle, likely, killed in battle because of David's sin.

157
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:29,740
This is the backdrop of our text.

158
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:30,440
And he says,

159
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:38,540
Have mercy on me, O God, according to your unfailing love, according to your great compassion.

160
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:41,520
Blot out my transgressions.

161
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:52,940
David has committed some incredibly horrific sins to this point and spent much time trying to cover it up to avoid any potential consequences for his actions.

162
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:57,060
But when Nathan confronts them, he realizes what needs to be done.

163
00:11:57,060 --> 00:12:00,960
Just like when the physician laid out all the facts for my mom, it's like,

164
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:07,700
Hey, I know it's going to be painful to detach your Achilles, but if you want healing, if you want restoration, this needs to happen.

165
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:12,380
David is the most powerful man in Israel.

166
00:12:12,380 --> 00:12:17,440
So it's important here that we realize David realizes what needs to be done, but it's not the only option that he had.

167
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:21,660
Because David is the most powerful man in Israel, he could have kept the charade going, right?

168
00:12:22,080 --> 00:12:23,880
He could have had the prophet Nathan killed.

169
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:25,740
Been like, I don't know what happened to the prophet.

170
00:12:25,740 --> 00:12:27,400
We're good, right?

171
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:29,780
He could have continued to cover up.

172
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:40,740
But this turning back to God that David performs in the midst of his rebellion and sin is ultimately what makes him a man who is described as a man after God's own heart.

173
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:46,980
He realizes that the good life is not wrapped up in getting what we want or avoiding the consequences of our pain,

174
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:53,920
but it is ultimately wrapped up in our willingness to surrender, surrender to Abba, Father, the God of the universe.

175
00:12:53,920 --> 00:13:00,720
If we want true healing, if we want true restoration, if we want true abundant life that Jesus says he comes to offer,

176
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:04,500
it does not come through us managing happiness through our circumstances.

177
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:12,080
It comes by us standing before the God of grace and allowing him to be the Lord and leader of our life.

178
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:22,880
The satisfaction that he was chasing in this sexual encounter with an off-limits woman can only be found in the loving embrace of the one who made him.

179
00:13:23,880 --> 00:13:30,820
And this is as true for us today as it was for David on the roof of his palace when he first laid eyes on Bathsheba,

180
00:13:30,820 --> 00:13:34,480
as it was for Adam and Eve in the cool of the Eden garden.

181
00:13:34,480 --> 00:13:37,880
We constantly have this choice laid before us.

182
00:13:37,880 --> 00:13:41,680
Are we going to trust that God is a good father who wants good things for us?

183
00:13:41,680 --> 00:13:47,760
Or are we going to take from him what is never intended to be ours?

184
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:49,920
This is Adam and Eve's sin.

185
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:51,680
This is David's sin on the roof.

186
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:53,540
And this is our struggle today.

187
00:13:53,620 --> 00:13:58,680
Are we going to trust that God is a loving father who ultimately wants what's best for us?

188
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:04,320
Or are we going to take from him what we are claiming is ours and use it for ourself?

189
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:07,540
We chase happiness, fulfillment, and satisfaction in a lot of places,

190
00:14:07,540 --> 00:14:11,500
but apart from an authentic relationship with God who made us and loved us,

191
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:13,480
we will always be left wanting.

192
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:16,380
Do not allow yourself.

193
00:14:16,380 --> 00:14:20,940
Now, even before we need to come into confession, I want to say,

194
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:25,520
do not allow yourself to be lured into destruction by the counterfeit joys of this world.

195
00:14:27,140 --> 00:14:34,100
So many people in my life have destroyed families,

196
00:14:34,100 --> 00:14:40,840
have caused irrevocable damage

197
00:14:40,840 --> 00:14:45,280
with one decision

198
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:50,300
in a secret place where they thought nobody would know and nobody would find out,

199
00:14:50,380 --> 00:14:53,720
and I will be satisfied and I will be fulfilled and I will be happy.

200
00:14:53,720 --> 00:14:58,440
And they spend the rest of their life taking the broken pieces of their lives back to God

201
00:14:58,440 --> 00:14:59,660
and asking him to rebuild him.

202
00:14:59,660 --> 00:15:03,160
And the beautiful part about this psalm is that he will and that he does,

203
00:15:03,160 --> 00:15:04,820
but it is not easy.

204
00:15:04,820 --> 00:15:05,880
It is painful.

205
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:10,920
And so before you even get there, make the decision, make the intentional effort

206
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:13,820
to seek after God, to seek obedience to him.

207
00:15:13,820 --> 00:15:16,100
Don't even get to this place.

208
00:15:16,100 --> 00:15:23,680
David knows that if he ever wants to experience a truly restored life,

209
00:15:23,680 --> 00:15:27,740
he must turn back to God, no matter how painful it might be.

210
00:15:27,780 --> 00:15:30,560
And he does so boldly, not rooted in who he is,

211
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:33,400
but rooted in the very character of God.

212
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:36,220
He says, forgive me, have mercy on me,

213
00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:38,420
according to your unfailing love,

214
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:40,300
according to your great compassion,

215
00:15:40,300 --> 00:15:43,080
blot out my transgressions.

216
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:45,300
Those of you who know your Bibles well,

217
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:48,480
likely are hearing echoes of Exodus 34, right?

218
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:51,660
God declaring his very core identity,

219
00:15:51,660 --> 00:15:54,140
his character, his nature to the people of Israel.

220
00:15:54,140 --> 00:15:55,440
He says, the Lord, the Lord,

221
00:15:55,560 --> 00:15:59,720
compassionate and gracious, slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love.

222
00:15:59,720 --> 00:16:04,180
This is who God declares himself to be to his people.

223
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:07,400
And David takes those words back to God

224
00:16:07,400 --> 00:16:10,520
and he declares to God, forgive me, not because of who I am.

225
00:16:10,520 --> 00:16:14,400
I don't know if you've spent much time in the history books,

226
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:16,680
but kings around this time were pretty arrogant.

227
00:16:16,680 --> 00:16:19,580
Many of them thought they were gods themselves.

228
00:16:19,580 --> 00:16:22,400
Like David very easily could have come into God's presence and said,

229
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:24,460
God, I am the king of your people.

230
00:16:24,620 --> 00:16:26,900
You need to forgive me for the sake of your name.

231
00:16:26,900 --> 00:16:28,920
He doesn't do that.

232
00:16:28,920 --> 00:16:30,580
He doesn't come into God's presence and say,

233
00:16:30,580 --> 00:16:33,020
because of who I am, I need your forgiveness.

234
00:16:33,020 --> 00:16:35,720
Because I'm the king, because you've anointed me,

235
00:16:35,720 --> 00:16:38,140
because you've put me in this place, I need your forgiveness.

236
00:16:38,140 --> 00:16:40,420
He doesn't say, because I've deserved it.

237
00:16:40,420 --> 00:16:41,860
He doesn't say, because of who I am.

238
00:16:41,860 --> 00:16:44,100
He says, because of who you are.

239
00:16:46,180 --> 00:16:49,580
Confession has to first and foremost be rooted in the character of God,

240
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:52,060
who is eager to forgive.

241
00:16:52,060 --> 00:16:56,920
It's reminding me of the story of the prodigal son.

242
00:16:56,920 --> 00:17:01,080
The moment the father sees the son moving towards him, he sprints.

243
00:17:01,740 --> 00:17:04,480
This is the character of the God that we serve.

244
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:06,780
He is eager to forgive.

245
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:12,640
Many of us in the room, we likely don't have the level of sin that David has at this point,

246
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:13,660
although some of us might.

247
00:17:15,220 --> 00:17:18,040
We've got some detectives as a part of our congregation.

248
00:17:18,040 --> 00:17:21,640
So if you want to come forward and make some confessions after service, let me know.

249
00:17:21,640 --> 00:17:23,060
We'll take you down to the station.

250
00:17:23,060 --> 00:17:23,900
We'll figure it out.

251
00:17:23,900 --> 00:17:28,840
But most of us probably don't have this level of sin in our lives.

252
00:17:28,840 --> 00:17:31,680
But we're all capable of it.

253
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:34,180
And the moment that we think we're not,

254
00:17:34,180 --> 00:17:39,140
we're all capable of it.

255
00:17:39,140 --> 00:17:41,160
And based on Christ's clarification of sin,

256
00:17:41,160 --> 00:17:43,420
he says, if we've lusted, we've committed adultery.

257
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:46,780
And if we've gossiped out of hatred, we've committed murder.

258
00:17:46,780 --> 00:17:51,440
All of these sins cost so much damage to ourselves and those around us.

259
00:17:51,440 --> 00:17:54,540
And we've likely all done both of those things and covered them up

260
00:17:54,540 --> 00:17:58,300
and justified them and made excuses as to why it's not that big of a deal.

261
00:17:58,300 --> 00:18:02,880
And until we're truly honest with ourselves and with God and confession,

262
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:06,660
we will never be healed at the source of our pain and our sorrow.

263
00:18:06,660 --> 00:18:08,840
I love how Tim Keller puts it.

264
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:11,000
You guys have probably heard, some of you have heard this quote before.

265
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,400
This is the gospel.

266
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:16,980
We are more sinful and flawed in ourselves than we ever dared believe.

267
00:18:16,980 --> 00:18:20,420
And yet at the very same time,

268
00:18:20,420 --> 00:18:24,280
we are more loved and accepted in Jesus Christ than we ever dared hope.

269
00:18:24,280 --> 00:18:31,880
We have to be honest with ourselves before God about the brokenness of our sin.

270
00:18:32,380 --> 00:18:36,640
And yet at the same time, we don't come into his presence with shame.

271
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:43,120
We come into his presence knowing that he loves us and accepts us in the midst of our failure and our brokenness.

272
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:55,440
David roots his confession in God's character.

273
00:18:55,440 --> 00:19:01,080
But confession isn't as easy as we might think it is, right?

274
00:19:01,080 --> 00:19:02,800
Like on the surface, confession sounds easy.

275
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:07,120
Like, oh, I'll just jog into God's presence and just tell him all the things I did.

276
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:08,580
He'll forgive me and we'll move on.

277
00:19:10,460 --> 00:19:14,220
But it's a painful participation in grace.

278
00:19:14,220 --> 00:19:16,560
Forgiveness is freely given by God.

279
00:19:16,560 --> 00:19:22,420
But genuine confession is a costly process of rooting out our own rebellion, our idolatry.

280
00:19:22,580 --> 00:19:24,900
And it takes honest self-reflection.

281
00:19:24,900 --> 00:19:29,560
It takes us choosing to say the same thing about our sin that God says about our sin.

282
00:19:29,560 --> 00:19:37,600
And we live in a culture that would rather downplay the realities of our failure and pump up the realities of our success.

283
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:43,440
We take credit for the crowns and we diminish the sin.

284
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:49,340
And confession is a place where we get honest with ourselves and with others before God.

285
00:19:49,340 --> 00:19:50,660
And we say, this is who I am.

286
00:19:52,580 --> 00:19:57,620
And I'm here knowing that I'm loved by the Father.

287
00:19:57,620 --> 00:20:05,320
But if I'm ever going to find true healing and restoration moving forward, I have to be honest with what's really going on in my life.

288
00:20:05,320 --> 00:20:13,740
David opens up his soul to God's operating table.

289
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:19,360
He finally begins to acknowledge the realities of his sinful state and his sinful actions before a holy God.

290
00:20:19,360 --> 00:20:19,960
Let's read it.

291
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:20,480
Verse 2.

292
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:24,920
Wash away all my iniquities and cleanse me from my sin.

293
00:20:24,920 --> 00:20:29,500
For I know my transgressions and my sin is always before me.

294
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:34,040
Does this sound like an easy thing to write about yourself?

295
00:20:34,040 --> 00:20:40,940
Against you and you only have I sinned and done what is evil in your sight.

296
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:44,560
So you are right in your verdict and you are justified when you judge.

297
00:20:44,780 --> 00:20:48,860
Surely I was sinful at birth, sinful from the time my mother conceived me.

298
00:20:48,860 --> 00:20:51,520
Yet you desired faithfulness even in the womb.

299
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:53,400
You taught me wisdom in the secret place.

300
00:20:53,400 --> 00:20:55,440
Cleanse me with hyssop and I will be clean.

301
00:20:55,440 --> 00:20:57,720
Wash me and I will be whiter than snow.

302
00:20:57,720 --> 00:21:00,160
Let me hear joy and gladness.

303
00:21:00,160 --> 00:21:02,300
Let the bones you have crushed rejoice.

304
00:21:02,300 --> 00:21:07,580
Hide your face from my sins and blot out all my iniquity.

305
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:15,220
So David opens up his soul to God's operating table.

306
00:21:15,220 --> 00:21:19,700
He's finally beginning to acknowledge the reality of his sin before a holy God.

307
00:21:19,700 --> 00:21:21,480
And he uses this really peculiar language.

308
00:21:21,480 --> 00:21:30,600
And it's not really a part of my main point, but I feel like I have to address it because it jumps off the page to you if you are a victim of any sin of someone else.

309
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:33,180
He says, against you and you only have I sinned.

310
00:21:33,180 --> 00:21:35,440
Is anybody else like, hands up?

311
00:21:35,440 --> 00:21:49,720
Uriah, Bathsheba, the nation of Israel, the other soldiers that were at war with Uriah, their wives and their children.

312
00:21:49,800 --> 00:21:55,840
Like, we could make a laundry list of people that David has offended with his sin.

313
00:21:55,840 --> 00:22:01,720
The consequences of those decisions will impact him and others for the rest of his life.

314
00:22:01,720 --> 00:22:09,340
But what David gets here is he says that sin, sin is ultimately only against God because he is the one who defines right and wrong.

315
00:22:10,340 --> 00:22:15,020
He is the only one who can truly be a just judge according to his standard.

316
00:22:15,020 --> 00:22:20,020
And he is the only one that we can be certain of will forgive us.

317
00:22:22,160 --> 00:22:25,080
David can't expect forgiveness from Bathsheba.

318
00:22:25,080 --> 00:22:28,100
He can't expect forgiveness from the people that he has wronged.

319
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:29,080
He should seek it.

320
00:22:29,080 --> 00:22:32,740
But people are people and they may withhold forgiveness.

321
00:22:32,740 --> 00:22:42,220
David's sin is ultimately before God and he can be confident and assured that even if the people that he has offended never forgive him, that he can find forgiveness and healing in God.

322
00:22:45,740 --> 00:22:54,520
We need to be a people who regularly go to God in confession and bear our true selves before him to receive his grace.

323
00:22:54,520 --> 00:23:04,000
It's important here that we realize that David isn't just confessing these actions, his horrific actions in the moment.

324
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:10,540
But he actually uses the entire spectrum, the entire vocabulary that the Hebrew language has for sin.

325
00:23:10,540 --> 00:23:14,980
David isn't just confessing the actions that he committed against Bathsheba and Uriah.

326
00:23:15,080 --> 00:23:20,160
He's actually just confessing all of his sin before a holy God.

327
00:23:20,160 --> 00:23:25,880
And if you will be patient with me, can we stumble through some Hebrew for a couple of minutes?

328
00:23:25,880 --> 00:23:27,240
Okay.

329
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:28,520
I need your patience.

330
00:23:28,520 --> 00:23:29,400
I need your grace.

331
00:23:29,400 --> 00:23:30,280
I have to be honest.

332
00:23:30,280 --> 00:23:34,340
I feel a little bit more confident, a little bit more confident when we get into the Greek.

333
00:23:34,340 --> 00:23:38,620
I did pass two semesters of seminary Greek with like a C minus.

334
00:23:38,620 --> 00:23:42,180
So I feel better about that.

335
00:23:42,180 --> 00:23:45,220
Hebrew I dropped out of three weeks into my freshman semester.

336
00:23:45,220 --> 00:23:47,720
So we're going to stumble together.

337
00:23:47,720 --> 00:23:48,520
Does that sound good?

338
00:23:48,520 --> 00:23:52,100
I referenced a lot of Tim Mackey here.

339
00:23:52,100 --> 00:23:52,900
I trust him.

340
00:23:52,900 --> 00:23:55,080
And so we're going to stumble through some Hebrew.

341
00:23:55,080 --> 00:23:56,120
You're going to be patient with me.

342
00:23:56,120 --> 00:24:01,120
And at the end, I hope that it really adds some texture to what David is saying here.

343
00:24:01,340 --> 00:24:10,740
David primarily uses three Hebrew nouns to describe his sin and three verbs used in the Torah about God's forgiveness of sin.

344
00:24:11,160 --> 00:24:14,800
That makes the people acceptable to enter the tabernacle of worship.

345
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:33,860
Now, before we even get into this, we have to remind ourselves that the law and the sacrificial system in the Old Testament was given to show God's people that a holy and righteous judge who we have all sinned against, who we have all expelled ourselves out of his presence, that this holy and righteous judge is moving towards his sinful people.

346
00:24:34,360 --> 00:24:36,880
He's moving towards the brokenness of creation.

347
00:24:36,880 --> 00:24:40,040
He is making a way for them to be acceptable into his presence.

348
00:24:40,040 --> 00:24:42,740
He is restoring the brokenness of their world.

349
00:24:42,740 --> 00:24:44,840
And that is the story of the Bible.

350
00:24:46,260 --> 00:24:50,380
Now, we look at the Old Testament, and it looks messy, and it looks raw, and it looks weird.

351
00:24:50,380 --> 00:24:51,500
And we're like, what's going on there?

352
00:24:51,500 --> 00:24:52,920
The story's not finished yet.

353
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:55,220
God is moving towards his people.

354
00:24:55,220 --> 00:24:58,240
He's moving towards the brokenness of creation.

355
00:24:58,240 --> 00:24:59,300
And so there's some language.

356
00:24:59,300 --> 00:25:05,960
There's a sacrificial system set up to show God's people that God is moving towards them in the midst of their brokenness.

357
00:25:05,960 --> 00:25:11,560
We've got three words for sin, three words for this cleansing, forgiveness, blotting out.

358
00:25:12,160 --> 00:25:14,800
The first is Pasha.

359
00:25:14,800 --> 00:25:17,140
Everybody say Pasha.

360
00:25:17,140 --> 00:25:21,980
We're probably not pronouncing that right, so don't say it in front of, like, an Orthodox Jew or anything.

361
00:25:21,980 --> 00:25:25,700
But we've got it good enough to know what we're talking about.

362
00:25:25,700 --> 00:25:29,260
Pasha means, it's translated transgressions in the passage here.

363
00:25:29,260 --> 00:25:32,500
It is willful and active rebellion against God.

364
00:25:33,680 --> 00:25:37,960
This is David's sin against Bathsheba, his murder of Uriah.

365
00:25:37,960 --> 00:25:42,260
He's saying, I have willfully and actively committed rebellion against you, God.

366
00:25:42,260 --> 00:25:49,480
And the result of unconfessed, unforgiven Pasha is ultimately God's judgment, his wrath.

367
00:25:52,120 --> 00:25:54,900
We've all sinned and fallen short of God's glory.

368
00:25:54,900 --> 00:25:57,700
The wages of sin is death.

